<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policymap [
<!ELEMENT policymap (policy)*>
<!ATTLIST policymap xmlns CDATA #FIXED "">
<!ELEMENT policy EMPTY>
<!ATTLIST policy xmlns CDATA #FIXED "">
<!ATTLIST policy domain NMTOKEN #REQUIRED>
<!ATTLIST policy name NMTOKEN #IMPLIED>
<!ATTLIST policy pattern CDATA #IMPLIED>
<!ATTLIST policy rights NMTOKEN #IMPLIED>
<!ATTLIST policy stealth NMTOKEN #IMPLIED>
<!ATTLIST policy value CDATA #IMPLIED>
]>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only

  ImageMagick security policy for the app image.

  This is the OTHER half of the guard on rider photo uploads. PhotoProcessor
  sets its own resource limits and refuses an oversized canvas on the header
  (docs/specs/photo-uploads.md §7), but that is application code: it protects
  the path it runs on. This file protects the library itself, including any
  code path that reaches ImageMagick without going through PhotoProcessor.

  Debian's stock policy is not bad — it carries resource limits and denies the
  URL/HTTP coders — but it leaves every other coder readable and every delegate
  executable. The coder families below are the ImageTragick ones: MVG and MSL
  are scripting languages ImageMagick will happily interpret out of a file that
  claims to be an image, and EPHEMERAL/URL turn a decode into a file read or an
  outbound request. The application already refuses anything that is not
  JPEG/PNG/WebP/HEIC after decoding; this refuses them before.

  Deny-all-then-allow is deliberate. An allowlist that names the risks has to
  be edited every time ImageMagick gains a coder; this one is closed by default
  and lists the five formats we actually accept.

  NO BACKTICKS IN THIS FILE, not even inside a comment.

  ImageMagick does not read policy.xml with a standards-compliant XML parser;
  it uses its own, and that parser treats a backtick as a quote character.
  Measured on 2026-08-03 against ImageMagick 7.1.1: the first version of this
  file used backticks for code spans in two comments, and the ENTIRE coder
  allowlist below them was silently ignored — GIF decoded happily, with no
  error, no warning and no log line. Exactly which placements break it was not
  worth pinning down; the rule is simply that they are banned here.

  That is the failure mode this whole arrangement is built around: a bad policy
  file is not an error, it is a policy that quietly does nothing. So the image
  build ASSERTS the policy is in effect (web/Dockerfile) rather than trusting
  that the file was copied. A guard you believe in but do not have is worse
  than no guard, because you stop looking.
-->
<policymap>
  <!-- Anything not named is refused. -->
  <policy domain="Undefined" rights="none"/>

  <!--
    Resource ceilings. These are hard maxima: PHP's own
    Imagick::setResourceLimit() calls cannot raise them, only lower, which is
    the relationship we want — the app's limits are a floor it sets for
    itself, and these are the ones an attacker cannot argue with.

    The area limit must stay ABOVE PhotoProcessor::MAX_PIXELS (50 MP), or a legitimate
    large photo would spill to the disk cache on every upload instead of being
    handled in memory. width/height match RESOURCE_LIMITS.
  -->
  <policy domain="resource" name="memory" value="512MiB"/>
  <policy domain="resource" name="map" value="1GiB"/>
  <policy domain="resource" name="area" value="64MP"/>
  <policy domain="resource" name="disk" value="1GiB"/>
  <policy domain="resource" name="width" value="30KP"/>
  <policy domain="resource" name="height" value="30KP"/>
  <policy domain="resource" name="time" value="60"/>
  <policy domain="resource" name="thread" value="2"/>
  <!-- We process one still image at a time; a "sequence" of thousands of
       frames is not something a rider photo has any reason to be. -->
  <policy domain="resource" name="list-length" value="32"/>

  <!-- Indirect reads (@file) are how a coder is talked into reading the
       filesystem. Nothing we do needs them. -->
  <policy domain="path" rights="none" pattern="@*"/>
  <!-- No external programs. HEIC is decoded by libheif, which is linked into
       ImageMagick and is NOT a delegate, so this costs us nothing. -->
  <policy domain="delegate" rights="none" pattern="*"/>
  <policy domain="filter" rights="none" pattern="*"/>
  <policy domain="module" rights="none" pattern="{MSL,MVG,PS,EPS,PDF,SVG,URL,XPS,EPHEMERAL,TEXT,SHOW,WIN,PLT}"/>

  <!-- The allowlist that matters. Same five formats PhotoProcessor accepts
       after decoding, refused here before one is attempted. -->
  <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="*"/>
  <policy domain="coder" rights="read|write" pattern="{JPEG,PNG,WEBP,HEIC,HEIF}"/>
</policymap>
